                    "presentation_state": "show_all",
                    "properties": {
                        "0": {
                            "name": "block",
                            "value": "Finds, element by element, the least common multiple of two tensors of whole numbers: the smallest number both divide into exactly.\n\nThe lcm of 4 and 6 is 12. In a patch it answers \"when do these line up again?\": two loops of 4 and 6 beats coincide every 12 beats, so the lcm of two cycle lengths is the length of the combined pattern before it repeats.\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.lcm\nIt takes no arguments.\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\ntensor a in:\nThe first tensor, list or number. Receiving it does the work.\n\ntensor b in:\nThe second tensor, list or a single number used for every element. It is only stored when it arrives, so send it FIRST; until it has received something the node sends nothing.\n\nThe shapes must broadcast: the same shape, a single number, or shapes that differ only where one has length 1. The demo's table is exactly that - a column of 12 against a row of 12 gives all 144 pairs.\n\nWHOLE NUMBERS ONLY:\nThis is number theory, not arithmetic. Both inputs are turned into whole numbers (64-bit integers) before the work is done, so lists and float tensors can go straight in. A value with a fraction is rounded to the NEAREST whole number first: 2.6 counts as 3 and 9.4 as 9, so a value like 11.9999 that should have been 12 does not quietly become 11. For a different rule put t.floor or t.ceil in front.\n\nZERO:\nThe lcm of anything with 0 is 0 (0 is the only multiple of 0).\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nlcm tensor out:\nThe least common multiples, as 64-bit integers, in the broadcast shape. They grow quickly - the lcm of 11 and 12 is already 132.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nTop: four pairs at once. 12 and 8 give 24, 18 and 12 give 36, 7 and 21 give 21, 9 and 0 give 0.\n\nBottom: every pair from 1 to 12. Row r, column c holds the lcm of r+1 and c+1 (the top-left cell is 1 and 1). Pairs with nothing in common multiply out in full and glow brightest towards the bottom right (11 and 12 give 132); pairs that share factors stay dark, and the diagonal - a number with itself - is only the number.\n\nRELATED:\nt.gcd is the other half: the largest number dividing both. For any pair, gcd times lcm is the pair multiplied together. t.round and t.floor make whole numbers from fractions.",
                            "value_type": "str"
                        },
                        "1": {
                            "name": "lock",
                            "value": true,
                            "value_type": "bool"
                        },
                        "2": {
                            "name": "width",
                            "value": 760,
                            "value_type": "int"
                        },
                        "3": {
                            "name": "height",
                            "value": 885,
                            "value_type": "int"
                        },
                        "4": {
                            "name": "text_size",
                            "value": "30",
                            "value_type": "str"
                        }
                    },
                    "text": "Finds, element by element, the least common multiple of two tensors of whole numbers: the smallest number both divide into exactly.\n\nThe lcm of 4 and 6 is 12. In a patch it answers \"when do these line up again?\": two loops of 4 and 6 beats coincide every 12 beats, so the lcm of two cycle lengths is the length of the combined pattern before it repeats.\n\nSYNTAX:\nt.lcm\nIt takes no arguments.\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\ntensor a in:\nThe first tensor, list or number. Receiving it does the work.\n\ntensor b in:\nThe second tensor, list or a single number used for every element. It is only stored when it arrives, so send it FIRST; until it has received something the node sends nothing.\n\nThe shapes must broadcast: the same shape, a single number, or shapes that differ only where one has length 1. The demo's table is exactly that - a column of 12 against a row of 12 gives all 144 pairs.\n\nWHOLE NUMBERS ONLY:\nThis is number theory, not arithmetic. Both inputs are turned into whole numbers (64-bit integers) before the work is done, so lists and float tensors can go straight in. A value with a fraction is rounded to the NEAREST whole number first: 2.6 counts as 3 and 9.4 as 9, so a value like 11.9999 that should have been 12 does not quietly become 11. For a different rule put t.floor or t.ceil in front.\n\nZERO:\nThe lcm of anything with 0 is 0 (0 is the only multiple of 0).\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nlcm tensor out:\nThe least common multiples, as 64-bit integers, in the broadcast shape. They grow quickly - the lcm of 11 and 12 is already 132.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nTop: four pairs at once. 12 and 8 give 24, 18 and 12 give 36, 7 and 21 give 21, 9 and 0 give 0.\n\nBottom: every pair from 1 to 12. Row r, column c holds the lcm of r+1 and c+1 (the top-left cell is 1 and 1). Pairs with nothing in common multiply out in full and glow brightest towards the bottom right (11 and 12 give 132); pairs that share factors stay dark, and the diagonal - a number with itself - is only the number.\n\nRELATED:\nt.gcd is the other half: the largest number dividing both. For any pair, gcd times lcm is the pair multiplied together. t.round and t.floor make whole numbers from fractions."
                },
